design:
Use provider-neutral rules records shaped like `{ systemId, kind, key, name, data, source, license, overrides? }`. Keep `data` as `unknown` at this layer; system-specific validators interpret it later. Addon packs must declare compatible base systems and versions in metadata. The license type should either wrap or structurally mirror the existing world `PackLicense`, but this task must not move world code unless that is the smallest clean change.

Initial test sketch:
```ts
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { assertShippableRulesPack, validateRulesPack, RulesPackError } from '../src/index.js';

it('validates a base D&D rules pack with license metadata', () => {
  const pack = validateRulesPack(validRulesPack({ role: 'base', systemId: 'dnd5e-srd' }));
  expect(pack.meta.systemId).toBe('dnd5e-srd');
});

it('rejects duplicate record keys in one pack', () => {
  const pack = validRulesPack({ records: [record('creature:goblin'), record('creature:goblin')] });
  expect(() => validateRulesPack(pack)).toThrow(RulesPackError);
});

it('rejects user-private packs at shippable boundaries', () => {
  const pack = validateRulesPack(validRulesPack({ licenseClass: 'user-private' }));
  expect(() => assertShippableRulesPack(pack.meta.license)).toThrow(RulesPackError);
});
```
